--- body ---
## Context

Author ruling, 2026-11-10, verbatim: "The embedded PDF titles are
unreliable". It answers a finding gaze raised twice, non-blocking, in the
reviews of tickets 1502 and 1503: `check-staging` can still call a file
`absent` from a title search alone, when the PDF has a real-looking embedded
title and its identifiers miss.

Today (`audit_one`, ~line 2572 on 2026-11-10; `_staging_verdict`, ~2528) a
title built from the **filename** that finds nothing is `unidentified`,
never `absent` (ticket 1456). A title read from the PDF **container**
(`_pdf_info`, ~2511) is trusted more: a miss on it can conclude `absent`,
and the report's action for `absent` is to create an item. Container titles
are whatever the producing tool wrote: a publisher's template string, the
title of another document, a truncated or mis-encoded line. Ticket 0081 of
sen-causal-reading was exactly this: a publisher string in /Title killed the
search for 15 files.

## Decision

A title taken from the file itself, whether from the container or from the
filename, is a search hint. A search on it may return candidates; a miss on
it alone is `unidentified`, never `absent`. Only a title someone **gave**
(a `.bib` entry, a receipt's given metadata) can, with no hit, conclude
`absent`. This is the same line 1502 and 1503 drew for identifiers: given
versus scraped.

No filtering of "junk" titles by pattern: the rule is about where the title
came from, not what it looks like.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A PDF with a plausible embedded title that matches nothing, and no given metadata, is `unidentified`; a test shows it, with a control where a `.bib` title that matches nothing is still `absent`
- [ ] The existing filename-title behaviour is unchanged
- [ ] `skills/zotero/references/` text on verdicts agrees with the code
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Plan (raid 13f6d8)

Line numbers are those of 2026-11-10; anchor by text, 1510 shifts them.

### Relevant files

- `scripts/zotero.py` — `audit_one` only: the `filename_only` assignment and
  the comment above it. `_staging_verdict`, `EXHAUSTIVE_KEYS`, `find_work`,
  `_pdf_info`, the filename retry and `RECONCILE_ACTIONS` stay as they are.
- `tests/test_zotero_check_staging_bib.py` — the red test and its control
  (fast tier).
- `tests/test_zotero_find_work.py` — the live twin (`integration`), after
  `test_the_filename_retry_leaves_bib_titled_and_unmatched_rows_alone`.
- `skills/zotero/references/backfill.md` — the `unidentified` row of the
  verdict table, the paragraph "A filename-derived title is a search hint",
  and steps 2 and 3 of the lookup order ("a real title, the `.bib` one or the
  PDF's own").
- `skills/zotero/references/helper-script.md` — the `check-staging` bullet and
  the paragraph after it, same two statements.

### Actions

1. In `audit_one`, a title is given only when the `.bib` entry supplies it and
   it was the title searched: `title_given = bool(bib_entry.get("title")) and
   not filename_title`; then `filename_only = not title_given and not any(key
   in res["consulted"] for key in EXHAUSTIVE_KEYS)`. A consulted given
   identifier still lets a miss say `absent`. Keep the name `filename_only` and
   the `_staging_verdict` signature (`tests/test_zotero_import.py` calls it by
   keyword); rewrite the comment to say a title taken from the file, container
   or filename, is a hint.
2. Receipt titles are not wired as given; no title is filtered by pattern;
   `find-work --metadata` is not touched (residual, named in the request).
3. Reference text as listed above, with one sentence saying a receipt's title
   is not given either, and why, and one saying what the author ruled: an
   embedded title is used, cautiously; on a row left `unidentified` by its
   embedded title the reading agent judges from the title and the file, no
   pattern does.

### Test

- First, red before the change, in `tests/test_zotero_check_staging_bib.py`:
  `scan0001.pdf` (a filename giving fewer than `TITLE_MIN_WORDS` words, or the
  test is green today through the filename retry), an embedded title of five
  words, `find_work` stubbed at the seam that file already stubs to return no
  match with `consulted == ["title"]`; `audit_one` must answer `unidentified`.
  Today it answers `absent`: an AssertionError on the verdict.
- Control, same file shape: the same title given by a `.bib` entry is `absent`;
  an embedded title beside a `.bib` DOI that misses is `absent`.
- Live twin with the real client: `make_pdf(tmp_path / "scan0001.pdf", ...,
  {"Title": ...})` is `unidentified`, the same title from a `.bib` entry is
  `absent`.
- Exit criterion 2 is shown by existing tests left untouched and green:
  `test_filename_only_no_match_is_unidentified`,
  `test_check_staging_verdicts_cover_every_answer`,
  `test_check_staging_retries_with_the_filename_when_the_embedded_title_is_junk`,
  `test_the_filename_retry_leaves_bib_titled_and_unmatched_rows_alone`.
- No test asserts the wording of a reference file.
- Live tests skip in a shell with no display. By hand:
  `env -u ZOTERO_NO_LAUNCH -u CI DISPLAY=:0 python3 -m pytest
  tests/test_zotero_find_work.py -m integration -k "embedded or check_staging"`
  (the client draws on its own private Xvfb); quote the pass and skip counts in
  the request.

### Collisions with 1510

1510 extracts the given-versus-hint block of `audit_one` some fifty lines
above, edits `attach_present_works` and `cmd_suggest_attachments`, adds a
paragraph further down `backfill.md` and puts its tests in other files. No
shared hunk; either order merges.
